@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Egress tiers — cumulative network policy, credentials on their own axis

node "internal/egress" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "modeBuilders\nopen · allowlist · review" as TABLE <<app>>
  component "modeAliases + Canonical()\nbroker→open · firewall→allowlist\nproxy→review" as ALIAS <<app>>
  component "Options.Credentials\nbroker (default) | forward" as CREDS <<app>>
}

folder "Tiers — each adds to the one above" as TIERS <<host>> {
  component "open\nMITM only, no Squid,\nno upstream — direct egress" as T1 <<app>>
  component "allowlist\n+ Squid, method pin, DLP" as T2 <<app>>
  component "review\n+ interactive connection prompts" as T3 <<app>>
}

component "open + forward\nthe deliberate bypass:\nno MITM, bridge network" as BYPASS <<error>>
cloud "Registry / internet" as NET <<cloud>>

ALIAS ARROW_MAIN TABLE : resolved ONCE in BuildPlan
TABLE ARROW_MAIN TIERS
CREDS ARROW_OPTIONAL BYPASS : ""forward"" on the open tier
T1 ARROW_CLOUD NET
T2 ARROW_CLOUD NET
T3 ARROW_CLOUD NET

note right of CREDS
  **Why the axes had to split.** The modes used to conflate network
  policy with credential handling, which is why cursor and DinD both
  had to be carved out as target-name exceptions in ""cmd/proveo"".
  Modes now name the network tier only; credentials are independent.

  Every tier brokers — the key stays host-side and is injected at the
  MITM — **except** open + ""forward"", which is the one shape with
  nothing to inject.
end note

note bottom of T1
  **The open tier still needs the MITM.** Injection is only a guarantee
  while the injector is the sole egress path, so the agent sits on an
  ""--internal"" network behind ""proveo-egress"" even with no allowlist.
  ""PROVEO_EGRESS_UPSTREAM"" is left unset so the MITM egresses direct
  rather than through Squid.

  ""PROVEO_EGRESS_OPEN=1"" then makes the policy honest about the word
  'open': it drops layer A (the write pin) and the byte budget, while
  **keeping the exfil-sink denylist and the DLP secret scan**. An open
  network must not become an open channel for the key it was just
  handed. Without that flag the tier silently enforced an allowlist it
  claimed not to have.
end note

note bottom of BYPASS
  **Two cases injection cannot serve**, and they are the same case from
  two directions. A vendor pinning its TLS (cursor) cannot be
  intercepted, so the credential must arrive intact. DinD needs an
  internet-capable agent network, which reopens a path around the
  injector — so brokering there would be a guarantee on paper only.
  Both are declared per def in ""harness.manifest"" ""capabilities"", not
  special-cased by target name.

  **And it is the one posture with no record.** A plain bridge means no
  MITM and no Squid: the real key goes straight to the provider and
  nothing observes the exchange. So a provider's own 401 and an egress
  denial look identical from the harness's side — except that only the
  first can happen here. Reproducing such a failure under ""allowlist"" is
  what turns it into a flow record worth reading.
end note

note right of ALIAS
  **Canonicalize once, in ""BuildPlan"".** Every downstream consumer —
  builders, golden files, and ""PROVEO_EGRESS_MODE"" in the agent's own
  env — then sees the current name even when the caller passed a
  retired one. The alias warning says the meaning //changed//: anyone
  who relied on ""broker"" handing the real key to the container now
  needs ""--credentials forward"".
end note

note bottom of T3
  ""review"" shares ""allowlist""'s topology exactly; the tiers differ only
  in what the MITM does on a block, carried by ""PROVEO_EGRESS_REVIEW=1"".
  The consuming side is the PTY overlay — see
  ""_spec/internal/reviewgate/pty-review-proxy.puml"".
end note
note as BrokerPin #F4F9FB
  **Pinning one provider is not the same as reaching one.**

  The broker injects a single credential, so it must resolve exactly one
  provider. With several keys on the host it used to refuse — the agent
  then got the ""proveo-brokered"" sentinel and the provider answered
  401/403, which reads as a bad key rather than a proveo decision.

  A model id names its provider, so ""ARCHITECT/EDITOR/SMALL_MODEL""
  resolve it (""provider.ModelProvider"", including bare ids like ""gpt-5"",
  ""grok-4"", ""glm-4.6""). If those models disagree the pin is refused
  rather than guessed.

  **The Squid allowlist does NOT collapse onto that pin.** A
  non-vendor-locked harness allowlists every detected provider, because a
  session may switch model mid-run; narrowing reach to the injected
  provider would block it. Only a manifest ""provider:"" lock keeps the
  narrow allowlist — that is what a vendor lock means.
end note

@enduml
